- How would you adapt to a dynamic window size?
- What optimizations exist to avoid full re-scan on every batch?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of online quantile estimation using the P² algorithm, a space-efficient alternative to GK-style sketches. It tests practical knowledge of update mechanics, error behavior, and the trade-offs between approximation and exact computation in streaming contexts.

## Key Concepts
- The P² algorithm uses five fixed-position marker heights to approximate quantiles
- Piecewise-linear interpolation between markers yields quantile estimates
- Per-element updates are constant time with fixed memory overhead
- The algorithm provides guarantees that improve with larger sample sizes

## Code Example
```javascript
class P2Quantile {
  constructor(quantile = 0.5) {
    this.quantile = quantile;
    this.n = 0;
    this.markers = [0, 0, 0, 0, 0];
    this.positions = [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1].map(p => p * 2);
  }

  update(value) {
    this.n++;
    if (this.n < 5) {
      this.markers[this.n - 1] = value;
      this.markers.sort((a, b) => a - b);
      return;
    }

    let j = this.quantile < 0.5 ? 1 : 3;
    if (value >= this.markers[j]) j++;

    for (let i = j; i < 5; i++) this.positions[i]++;

    for (let i = 1; i < 4; i++) {
      const d = this.positions[i] - this.positions[i-1];
      const target = (i + 1) * this.n / 4;
      if ((d > 1 && this.positions[i] < target) || 
          (d < 1 && this.positions[i] > target)) {
        const sign = d > 1 ? 1 : -1;
        this.markers[i] += sign * Math.min(d, Math.abs(this.positions[i] - target));
        this.positions[i] += sign;
      }
    }
  }

  getQuantile() {
    return this.markers[2];
  }
}
```

## Error Analysis
The P² algorithm exhibits decreasing error as more elements are processed. For uniform distributions, error is minimal; for skewed distributions, error may be slightly higher but remains bounded. Larger batch sizes reduce the frequency of updates but do not fundamentally alter the error characteristics.

## Comparison to Sorted Buffer
Maintaining a full sorted buffer of size N provides exact median computation but requires O(N) memory and O(log N) insertion time per element. The P² algorithm trades this exactness for constant memory and constant update time, making it suitable for memory-constrained streaming applications where approximate quantiles suffice.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic data structures with tight amortized bounds in a streaming context, including handling duplicates and edge cases like K=0. It also probes practical trade-offs for sliding windows.

## Key Concepts
- Maintain counts and a running pair counter
- Special-case K=0 to avoid double counting
- Support batched inserts/removals within a fixed window

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowPairCounter {
  constructor(K) {
    this.K = K; this.cnt = new Map(); this.pairs = 0;
  }
  add(x) {
    const k = this.K;
    if (k === 0) this.pairs += (this.cnt.get(x) || 0);
    else {
      const a = this.cnt.get(x + k) || 0;
      const b = this.cnt.get(x - k) || 0;
      this.pairs += a + b;
    }
    this.cnt.set(x, (this.cnt.get(x) || 0) + 1);
  }
  remove(x) {
    const k = this.K;
    const cur = this.cnt.get(x) || 0;
    if (k === 0) this.pairs -= (cur - 1);
    else {
      const a = this.cnt.get(x + k) || 0;
      const b = this.cnt.get(x - k) || 0;
      this.pairs -= (a + b);
    }
    if (cur === 1) this.cnt.delete(x); else this.cnt.set(x, cur - 1);
  }
  batch(addArr, removeArr) {
    for (const x of removeArr) this.remove(x);
    for (const x of addArr) this.add(x);
  }
  exists() { return this.pairs > 0; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if window size N changes over time?
- What are cache/memory implications when values are large or negative?

## Why This Is Asked
Explores practical dynamic approximate all-pairs shortest paths (APSP) with shared structure; tests understanding of trade-offs between update costs and query time in batch-dynamic settings.

## Key Concepts
- Batch-dynamic graphs with nonnegative edge weights
- Hub-based distance sketches for approximate APSP
- Amortized analysis across multiple query pairs
- Trade-offs between approximation accuracy ε and computational efficiency
- Incremental shortest path algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
// Hub-based distance approximation: dist(u,v) ≈ min_h∈H [dist(u,h) + dist(h,v)]

class HubBasedAPSP {
  constructor(graph, epsilon) {
    this.k = Math.ceil((1/epsilon) * Math.log(graph.n));
    this.hubs = this.selectHubs(graph);
    this.distances = new Map(); // hub -> vertex distances
  }

  updateBatch(edges) {
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      this.distances.set(hub, this.incrementalDijkstra(hub, edges));
    }
  }

  query(u, v) {
    let minDist = Infinity;
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      const dist = this.distances.get(hub).get(u) + 
                   this.distances.get(hub).get(v);
      minDist = Math.min(minDist, dist);
    }
    return minDist;
  }
}
```
